00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:48.480
Before explaining watermarking, we want to
see how existing systems for detecting AI

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.760
generated content, work so far.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:56.880
As an example, we will take GPTZero, since
it is a very popular tool, made specifically

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:57.720
for teachers.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:03.840
If I take the last part of the text that ChatGPT
generated for that project proposal, then

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.580
I see that GPTZero will tell us that the text
is generated by AI.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:15.900
The idea behind GPTZero is to measure perplexity
and burstiness, because these two properties

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:20.400
of the text
vary between machine written content and human

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:20.820
text.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:26.520
The first measure is the perplexity, of which
you have maybe heard already in the context

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:27.660
of language modelling.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:32.880
It measures how unfamiliar a produced text
is for a model.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.880
In other words, it is the opposite of the
probability that a sentence was generated

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:39.900
by a language model.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:43.620
A high probability means a low perplexity.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:50.280
To compute the perplexity, we go word by word
and we compute how probable it is for a language

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.880
model to have generated it, given the previous
words.

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:56.880
But how to get to these probabilities?

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:03.600
The best part is, that to build a model that
measures the perplexity or probabilities of

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:09.900
another language model such as ChatGPT, one
does not need direct access to ChatGPT’s

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:12.600
weights – access which only OpenAI has.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:17.820
All one needs is a lot of ChatGPT outputs
(the more the better).

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:21.360
This is something that an API can deliver.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:29.040
Then, one needs to train a small language
model (such as GPT-2) to reproduce these ChatGPT

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:29.760
outputs.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:36.720
And a language model is by definition a model
capable of computing probabilities for next

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.180
words given previous words.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:45.600
So, after training, when applied to text produced
by ChatGPT, the surrogate model will predict

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:51.360
high probabilities for text produced by ChatPGT
and low ones for other kinds of text.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:56.040
And with it, we end up computing probabilities
for the whole sentence.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:05:01.080
By multiplying all these probabilities, we
get the probability of the whole sentence

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:03.480
to be generated by a language model.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:09.780
The perplexity is defined such, that a low
probability means a high perplexity for the

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:14.820
model, in other words, the language model
is surprised by the text we have given it,

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:18.240
since there are a lot of improbable words
in it,

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:22.980
and it would not have generated them, but
rather humans could have written it – or

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:24.300
other language models.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:30.540
So, GPTZero assumes that human written text
has a high perplexity for the language model.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:35.640
In turn, language model generated text would
have low perplexity,

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:41.160
because the high probability says that it
is exactly the kind of text the model would

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:41.880
have generated.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:48.480
The second property that GPTZero looks at
is the so-called “burstiness” which measures

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:50.040
the sentence complexity.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:56.160
Humans vary their sentences a lot when judging
by length and the amount of rare words they

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:01.320
use, so the ones on the long tail of the Zipfian
distribution that describes how frequently

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:02.340
we use words.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:08.280
Burstiness has something to do with the fact
that for example, rare words usually do not

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:12.720
occur very often in our writing, but when
they do, they start to happen a lot for a

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:14.760
sentence or two, then not anymore.

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:19.320
Language models are more constant in the way
they write out their sentences.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:24.420
So going on sentence by sentence, one can
plot the complexity of each sentence.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:30.720
For humans, these values will vary a lot,
while for models, the value will be quite

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:32.460
similar for all sentences.

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:39.120
Then a bumpy burstiness graph will likely
belong to a human essay, while a more constant

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:42.240
graph will belong to an AI generated essay.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:47.940
A problem with GPTZero is that it does not
always work and there are ways to fool it

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:53.040
into saying that AI-generated text is human
written, by introducing spelling mistakes

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:54.840
or tiny grammar errors.

00:06:54.840 --> 00:07:01.260
Also, what if your writing has such a low
burstiness (thus complexity), that GPTZero

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:03.540
says it is AI written?

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:06.300
Nobody wants to be accused of that!

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:10.800
Then how to detect AI generated text with
way more confidence?

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:16.500
With watermarking, where the maker of the
model decides to deliver its models’ text

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:21.420
output with a unique
fingerprint that is unnoticeable for humans,

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:25.800
but easily detectable with statistics, as
presented in this paper.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:30.840
But as it usually happens, good ideas come
from multiple places:

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:34.500
As highlighted in a tweet&nbsp;
by one of the watermarking&nbsp;

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:38.460
paper authors, OpenAI speaks about a similar

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:43.740
idea already, so we will not be surprised
to see watermarking coming to ChatGPT in the

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:43.920
future.

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:46.620
So, how does watermarking work?

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:52.380
It relies very much on what happens in a language
model when it chooses which word to predict

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:55.620
next, so on the so-called decoding mechanism.

00:07:55.620 --> 00:08:02.400
A language model (be it transformer-based
like GPT or on LSTMs like older language models

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:09.240
were) is just this thing that having a sequence,
possibly empty, of words, can predict the

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:11.100
probability for the next word.

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:17.640
But we want text with actual words, not a
bunch of probabilities for each word in the

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:18.540
English vocabulary!

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:24.360
This is where the decoding scheme comes in:
one way to come up with a word from a probability

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:30.360
distribution over the words, is to take the
most next probable word – this is called

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:31.560
greedy decoding.

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:36.180
But this is not a strategy modern languages
models use for decoding,

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:40.140
because ok, let’s suppose we have taken
the next most probable word.

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:44.700
Then at the next generation step with the
language model, we would take the input text,

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:49.320
the last prediction and based on this and
we compute again probability distributions

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:50.340
for the next word.

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:57.300
With greedy decoding, we would again take
the next most probable word, and so on, but

00:08:57.300 --> 00:09:02.160
this has been shown to produce very repetitive
and unsurprising text.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:04.440
We humans do not like that.

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:12.600
So there exist more interesting&nbsp;
decoding mechanisms,&nbsp;

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:15.540
where one also chooses less probable words

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:21.300
from time to time at some of the steps, but
ensures a good overall probability.

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:25.440
It is here, at the decoding&nbsp;
step where watermarking&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:27.780
comes in, so where we are sampling the next

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:31.020
word from the predicted probability distribution
from the model.

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:32.100
We have.

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:37.500
let’s say, 50 thousand English words the
language model knows about and predicts the

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:39.720
probability for, given the previous words.

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:47.100
But before choosing the next word, watermarking
randomly sets let’s say, 20% of all these

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:48.480
words to a blacklist.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:54.240
So the language model in decoding, can choose
only from the rest 80% of the words.

00:09:54.240 --> 00:10:00.900
The seed for the random number generator that
chooses which words are blacklisted, is the

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:02.340
last word of the input.

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:06.000
In this way, the blacklist can be reconstructed
at any time.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:12.120
This procedure is applied at each generation
of the next token, where for each next word,

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:18.660
the last word is used as a seed for randomly
blacklisting 20% of the words in

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:19.320
the vocabulary.

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:30.960
To detect generated text by this language
model, one needs to detect the watermark and

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:36.900
this is fairly easy: one goes through the
generated text and counts the blacklisted

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:37.140
words.

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:42.960
One gets the blacklist by knowing the random
number generator used to choose the blacklist

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:44.520
words and the seed.

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:49.740
The watermarked language model would not use
blacklisted words because it can’t,

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:53.520
but a human would definitely use blacklisted
terms.

00:10:53.520 --> 00:11:01.200
So a text using only whitelist words is highly
likely to be AI generated and even a short

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:04.140
text can be classified with relatively high
certainty.

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:10.440
But you will think that this watermarking
procedure will produce text that looks wrong

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:15.420
to humans, because some words just must be
used after others.

00:11:15.420 --> 00:11:22.380
When one says “The quick brown fox jumps”,
then of course THE plausible word there is

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:27.780
“over”, since this sentence is often used,
for example to show how fonts look like.

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:33.360
And if “over” gets blacklisted by random
sampling, then the language model will produce

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:33.780
garbage!

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:41.880
Well, the authors take care of this by choosing
when not to blacklist important stuff: If

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:46.080
the only high possibility to complete the
sentence is the word “over”, then the

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:50.280
probabilities computed by the language model
would look like this, with a high probability

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:53.340
for “over” and very low for others.

00:11:53.340 --> 00:12:00.300
This distribution has low entropy, since all
of the probability mass peaks on this word.

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:05.520
If there were many possibilities to complete
this phrase, then the language model’s output

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:11.280
will have high entropy, with the probabilities
being distributed fairly equally among many

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:17.160
other words So, the authors make sure to never
blacklist low entropy words, but rather high

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:22.080
entropy cases where the model can choose other
words that are just as good as the blacklisted

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:22.380
words.

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:25.260
And, this was watermarking in a nutshell.

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:33.060
The authors conduct experiments with an openly
accessible model, so OPT-1.3B, but there is

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:37.020
no reason to believe that this method would
not apply to ChatGPT too.

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:42.540
They see that the watermark does not hurt
model perplexity and that the model still

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:44.400
generates high quality text.

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:50.520
We are curious to see how if implemented in
language models, watermarking will be a reliable

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:52.440
algorithm with no way to fool…

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:57.480
Ms. Coffee Bean, of course there are ways
to fool the watermarking algorithm.

00:12:57.480 --> 00:12:59.220
If the watermarking algorithm uses the last&nbsp;

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:01.980
token as a random seed,&nbsp;
then anyone can reconstruct

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:05.880
the blacklist by using the seed and the same
random number generator.

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:11.460
Even if the seed is kept secret, attackers
can still brute-force their way to reconstruct

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:12.720
the blacklist.

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:17.640
If one has the blacklist, all one needs to
do, is introduce enough blacklist tokens,

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:22.500
which are words that humans would use, but
the language model is not allowed to.

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:27.900
But brute-forcing their way to the blacklist,
means that the attacker queries the API a

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:32.940
lot of times with the same input, in which
case, the API provider can monitor and detect

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:34.320
this malicious activity.

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:39.480
But now, let’s talk about cases of attacks,
where watermarking cannot discern whether

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:41.460
text was human or AI written.

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:45.120
One way to attack is by doing word substitutions.

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:50.820
If humans rewrite content by ChatGPT, then
the sentences rewritten by humans will not

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:52.740
be detected by watermarking.

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:57.720
But arguably, the text is not written by AI
anymore, it was just inspired by it.

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:01.560
Of course, the attacker could&nbsp;
also use a non-watermarked&nbsp;

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:05.100
model to paraphrase the outputs of a watermarked

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:05.460
model.

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:11.760
Changing small things, like adding spaces,
emojis or misspellings could also affect the

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:14.880
watermark detection, but&nbsp;
this could be circumvented&nbsp;

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:17.400
by careful application of text normalization,

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:20.760
where things like additional whitespaces are
removed.

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:24.960
But nobody wants to submit an essay full of
misspellings and emojis, right?

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:28.380
But a powerful attack is the “emoji attack”
[Goodside 2023].

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:34.080
It instructs the language model explicitly
to add emojis or exchange letters in its output.

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:39.540
If the language model is powerful enough (and
ChatGPT is), it will do so.

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:44.940
Even though the text quality looks bad at
first, the attacker can automatically remove

00:14:44.940 --> 00:14:45.780
the garbage.

00:14:45.780 --> 00:14:51.360
This would randomize the blacklist of words
following the emojis and would fool watermarking.

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:55.500
The authors do not have a solution against
this attack, except of training the language

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:59.160
model to refuse to follow such instructions
in the first place.

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:04.920
So we see, there are some ways to fool even
watermarking, but one could still argue that

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:08.100
a watermark is better than no protection.

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:13.320
But the clear minus of watermarking is that
it only applies when people and companies

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:16.860
are willing to watermark their language models
they produce.

00:15:16.860 --> 00:15:22.800
Tools like GPTZero might be less reliable
at detection, but they apply to language models

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:25.560
that are not willing to do watermarking.

00:15:25.560 --> 00:15:31.920
Since training language models becomes more
accessible, it is clear that not every language

00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:34.500
model out there will be watermarked in the&nbsp;

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:37.080
future, unless there will&nbsp;
be some strict regulation

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:37.620
about it.

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:38.880
What do you think?

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:42.900
Would you feel better if ChatGPT were to become
watermarked?

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:47.760
Or you think that is just unnecessary work
that the world doesn’t need?

00:15:47.760 --> 00:15:49.800
Let us know in the comments!

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:52.440
Thanks for watching and see you next time!

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:54.720
Okay, bye!

